<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      The complete permission set. INTERNET to reach Supabase, VIBRATE for the
      10 ms send tick. Everything else here is declared with a reason below, and
      the app still has no code path that could want contacts, location, an image
      picker or an ads SDK.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
    <!-- Android 13+ made notifications a runtime permission. Without this the
         request in src/lib/push.ts resolves as denied and the token never rings. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!--
      Voice notes, for members and providers alike.

      react-native-nitro-sound declares this in its own library manifest, so it
      would merge in whether or not it appeared here. It is declared here anyway,
      because scripts/verify-manifest.mjs treats ALLOWED as the *required* set
      precisely so that a library's permission cannot arrive as a surprise: this
      line is the app claiming it, not tolerating it.

      What uses it: Composer's record button, one recording at a time, started by
      a tap and stopped by a tap. What does not: there is no background capture,
      no always-on listening, no call recording, and nothing records while the app
      is not in the foreground. The recorder is stopped in the composer's unmount
      cleanup for that reason.

      This changes the Play Data Safety declaration. docs/play/listing.md §4 now
      carries an Audio row, and web/privacy.html says what is recorded, where it
      lives and how long for. Uploading a build that declares this without both of
      those being true is a policy violation rather than a paperwork nit.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <!-- LiveKit's communication audio device module adjusts call routing and
         volume. Normal permission; no prompt or user data access.
         https://docs.livekit.io/transport/media/publish/ -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />
    <!--
      LiveKit/Agora route calls to paired Bluetooth headsets on Android 11 and
      below through the legacy Bluetooth APIs. Android requires both legacy
      declarations to stop at API 30; Android 12+ uses BLUETOOTH_CONNECT from
      AndroidX Core-Telecom instead.
      https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/bluetooth/bt-permissions
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH"
        android:maxSdkVersion="30"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADMIN"
        android:maxSdkVersion="30"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />

    <!--
      Camera, for capturing a photo to send in chat (#8). Requested at runtime in
      Composer before launchCamera; declared here because react-native-image-picker
      requires the permission to be present once the app asks for it. Photo capture
      only — no video, no background use.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <!--
      Camera is optional hardware, not a requirement (#18). Without this, declaring
      CAMERA makes Play treat a camera as required and hides the app from
      camera-less devices (some tablets/Chromebooks/foldables) — which cuts against
      the large-screen posture. The photo-capture "+" is one optional entry point,
      so the app must install everywhere and simply not offer capture where there
      is no camera.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      Belt and braces for the Play Data Safety declaration: a future transitive
      dependency cannot reintroduce these through manifest merging. If any
      library ever tries, the merger removes it here instead of shipping it.
      (Should the app ever need image input, use the system photo picker's
      ACTION_PICK_IMAGES / PickVisualMedia, which needs no permission at all.)
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID" tools:node="remove" />
    <!--
      Required by the Cashfree PG SDK (recharge / User Choice Billing, ADR-0022).
      Its analytics path calls ConnectivityManager.getActiveNetworkInfo() WITHOUT
      a permission check (com.cashfree.pg.cf_analytics ...ContextUtils.getNetworkType),
      so stripping this throws SecurityException and crashes the checkout the
      instant Pay is tapped. Unlike Sentry — which contributed this permission too
      but permission-checks first and degrades to UNKNOWN — Cashfree hard-requires
      it, so the app now owns and declares it.
      docs: https://www.cashfree.com/docs/payments/online/mobile/android (SDK prerequisites)
      It is a normal (non-dangerous) permission: no runtime prompt, no personal
      data, nothing to surface on the Play Data Safety form.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" tools:node="remove" />
    <!--
      READ_MEDIA_IMAGES STAYS REMOVED, and that is a decision, not an oversight.

      The attach panel shows a strip of real recent photos (RecentMediaModule),
      which needs MediaStore access. The documented pattern at
      developer.android.com/about/versions/14/changes/partial-photo-video-access
      requests READ_MEDIA_IMAGES, READ_MEDIA_VIDEO and
      READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED together. We take only the third.

      READ_MEDIA_IMAGES is a Play RESTRICTED permission
      (support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/14115180). It may
      be requested only where "system pickers... are not sufficient for your app
      to provide core functionality", and it needs a Permissions Declaration
      Form. This app ships the system photo picker as its Photos tile, so the
      form would be arguing against our own UI, and a rejected declaration blocks
      releases.

      The cost of leaving it out is real and accepted: without it the system
      dialog offers no "Allow all", so the strip only ever shows photos the user
      has explicitly selected. The Photos tile remains the full-library route.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" tools:node="remove" />
    <!--
      Android 14+ partial photo access. API 34 only — it does not exist below,
      and RecentMediaModule reports "unsupported" there rather than falling back
      to a broader permission.

      NOT Play-restricted: the policy page above lists READ_MEDIA_IMAGES and
      READ_MEDIA_VIDEO as the permissions requiring a declaration, and does not
      list this one. It grants access to a user-chosen subset, which is why.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS" tools:node="remove" />
    <!--
      Also from the Cashfree PG SDK, for tap-to-pay card reads. This app takes
      UPI and web checkout only and never reads a card at the device, so the
      capability is dead weight — and a payments-adjacent permission on the Play
      listing that we would have to justify without using.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.NFC" tools:node="remove" />

    <!-- Core-Telecom self-managed calling (developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/telecom/self-managed) -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_OWN_CALLS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <!-- FGS type permissions required on API 34+ (developer.android.com/about/versions/14/changes/fgs-types-required) -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CAMERA" />

    <!--
      Android 11 (API 30) restricts package visibility. Without explicit <queries>
      declarations, intents to UPI apps resolve to nothing and the Cashfree SDK
      cannot detect installed UPI apps (Google Pay, PhonePe, Paytm, BHIM).

      doc: https://www.cashfree.com/docs/payments/online/mobile/android
      doc: https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility

      These are intent-based queries, not permissions; they do not appear in the
      Play Data Safety section and do not require user consent.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.google.android.apps.nbu.paisa.user" />
        <package android:name="com.phonepe.app" />
        <package android:name="net.one97.paytm" />
        <package android:name="in.org.npci.upiapp" />
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="upi" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
      android:name=".MainApplication"
      android:label="@string/app_name"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
      android:allowBackup="false"
      android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
      android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
      android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
      android:supportsRtl="true">
      <!--
        NO `screenOrientation`, deliberately, and this is the second position on
        it rather than an oversight.

        The app WAS locked to portrait, for a real and reproducible reason: with
        no orientation set, focusing any text field in a landscape window handed
        the whole screen to the input method's fullscreen "extract" editor — a
        bare white field, a grey ALL-CAPS action button and a coloured underline,
        with the app not shrunk beside it or dimmed behind it but gone. Not one
        token of the design system survived. Re-reproduced on API 34 with Gboard
        12.4.05 on 2026-08-26, so it was current behaviour and not a stale note.

        That lock put the app in breach of
        https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/large-screens, which asks apps
        not to impose orientation or resizability restrictions, and it cost real
        users on foldables and tablets a landscape they were entitled to.

        The cause is now fixed at its source rather than worked around.
        `ReactEditText` applies `EditorInfo.IME_FLAG_NO_FULLSCREEN` only when its
        `disableFullscreenUI` prop is true, and React Native defaults that prop to
        FALSE. Every text field in the app now goes through
        `src/components/TextField.tsx`, which forces it on and removes it from its
        own prop type so no call site can turn it back off.
        `__tests__/platform.test.ts` fails if anything imports React Native's
        `TextInput` directly again.

        Two earlier attempts, recorded so nobody repeats them:
          - A theme-level `android:imeOptions">flagNoExtractUi` on an
            `editTextStyle`. Does not work: `ReactEditText` assigns `imeOptions`
            programmatically from its props and overwrites the theme default.
            Built and reproduced unchanged.
          - A native `ReactTextInputManager` subclass. Unnecessary once the prop
            was found, and it would have collided on the "AndroidTextInput" view
            manager name.

        `configChanges` keeps its orientation and size entries, which matters more
        now than it did under the lock: rotation, split screen, freeform and a
        folding hinge all resize the window, and a restart rebuilds the surface.
        `Composer`'s draft is local state by decision (see the comment at
        src/components/Composer.tsx:47), so a restart costs somebody's half-typed
        message.
      -->
      <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|orientation|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|uiMode"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
        android:exported="true">
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent-filter>
        <!-- Return leg of the optional OAuth account link. See src/api/auth.ts. -->
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
            <data android:scheme="companionchat" android:host="auth-callback" />
        </intent-filter>
      </activity>

      <!--
        The notification small icon, and it is not optional.

        Without it firebase-messaging falls back to android:icon, and the status
        bar renders a small icon from its alpha channel only, which turned the
        two-bubble mark into a featureless white ring on the lock screen. Seen on
        a device, not deduced. `ic_notification` is the monochrome silhouette the
        adaptive icon already ships, which is exactly the shape this slot wants.

        The colour tints the icon and the app name in the shade. Coral, so a
        notification from this app is recognisable before it is read.

        `tools:replace` on the colour is required, and finding out why explained
        the white ring: react-native-firebase_messaging already declares this
        same meta-data as @color/white. So the library was tinting our launcher
        icon's alpha mask pure white, the two facts that produced the bug were
        both defaults, neither of them ours.
      -->
      <meta-data
        android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_icon"
        android:resource="@drawable/ic_notification" />
      <meta-data
        android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_color"
        android:resource="@color/notification_accent"
        tools:replace="android:resource" />

      <!-- Foreground service for active calls — keeps process alive in background.
           foregroundServiceType declares the maximum capability set for API 34+.
           (developer.android.com/develop/background-work/services/foreground-services) -->
      <service
          android:name=".CallForegroundService"
          android:exported="false"
          android:foregroundServiceType="phoneCall|microphone|camera" />

      <receiver
          android:name=".CallActionReceiver"
          android:exported="false">
          <intent-filter>
              <action android:name="com.companionchat.ACTION_ANSWER_CALL" />
              <action android:name="com.companionchat.ACTION_DECLINE_CALL" />
              <action android:name="com.companionchat.ACTION_HANG_UP" />
          </intent-filter>
      </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
